{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1786942196,"nonce":"dvUjwUpGxu+TTsIZ7EhSDNZvxto=","title":"**What is wrong.** The three personal-access-token operations are implemented, specified and unreachable. `POST /api/v1/api-tokens` mints, `GET /api/v1/api-tokens` lists and `DELETE /api/v1/api-tokens/{id}` revokes, and all three are `security: [sessionCookie]` in `api/openapi/openapi.yaml:4462-4545`. `internal/identity/api/tokens.go:44-45` states the reason in words — \"It requires a session: a token cannot enumerate its own siblings\" — and `internal/identity/service/tokens_test.go:306-327` enforces it, refusing a headless principal with `forbidden/token_requires_user`. The only thing in oto that holds an `oto_session` cookie is the browser, and the browser has no screen for any of the three. `web/src/api/endpoints.ts` wraps every other settings operation and wraps none of these; `qk.settings` (`web/src/api/keys.ts:105-130`) declares nine paths and none of them is tokens; `SECTIONS` in `web/src/routes/settings.tsx:17-22` is four entries — sources, channels, policies, tuning. `ApiToken` and `ApiTokenCreated` are already declared at `web/src/api/types.ts:207-208` and are imported by nothing outside `web/src/api/generated/`.","message":"**The demonstration.** A fresh deployment has exactly one PAT, printed once by `go run ./cmd/oto bootstrap`, and that subcommand cannot be run a second time: `internal/app/bootstrap.go:70-71` declares `ErrAlreadyBootstrapped` — \"this deployment already has an org; bootstrap runs once\". So an operator who loses that token, or who wants a second one for CI, has no supported path left. What works today is opening devtools on a logged-in `:5173` and calling `fetch(\"/api/v1/api-tokens\", {method:\"POST\", credentials:\"include\", …})` by hand, because the `HttpOnly` cookie the request needs is one no script can read and no other client can be handed (`web/src/api/session.tsx:6-14`). A capability whose only access path is the browser console is not a capability the product offers.\n\n**Why it matters.** The PAT is the credential for everything outside the browser — `just fire-alert`, `just stream`, every `curl` in `deploy/alertmanager/alertmanager.yml`'s header comment, and every script an operator writes against `session|pat` endpoints, which is most of §E's table (`docs/design/SPEC.md:1880-1895`). SPEC §R2 (`docs/design/SPEC.md:35`) makes the two credential kinds equals — \"Auth is a session cookie (local password) or a bearer PAT\" — but only one of them can be created after install. The revoke half is worse than the mint half: `internal/identity/api/tokens.go:190-208` documents revocation taking effect within a 60-second credential-cache TTL, which is a well-built response to a leaked token that nobody can currently invoke. And `last_used_at` is carried on every row (`api/openapi/openapi.yaml:9537-9540`) for a list screen that does not exist, so an operator cannot see which of their tokens is still live.\n\n**Where.** `api/openapi/openapi.yaml:4462-4545` (the three operations), `:9510-9552` (`ApiTokenDTO`), `:9555-9568` (`ApiTokenCreatedDTO`), `:10690-10707` (`CreateTokenRequest`); `internal/identity/api/tokens.go` (all three handlers, already written); `web/src/api/endpoints.ts` (no wrapper for any of them), `web/src/api/keys.ts:105-130` (no key), `web/src/api/queries.ts:240-303` (`FRESHNESS`, which every new key must appear in), `web/src/api/types.ts:207-208` (the types, unused), `web/src/routes/settings.tsx:17-22` (`SECTIONS`).\n\n**Scope.** medium — no server change at all; a settings section, one query key with its freshness declaration, three endpoint wrappers and the one-time-secret dialog pattern `SourcesSection.tsx` already establishes.\n\n**Done when.** An operator signed into the UI can mint a token, read its secret exactly once, see their existing tokens by prefix and last use, and revoke one — without leaving the product. The new key appears in `qk`, declares `mutation` in `FRESHNESS` and passes `queries.test.tsx`'s check that something actually invalidates it. The mint dialog says what the API says: the secret is shown once and only a hash is stored. The list shows only the caller's own tokens, which is what the service already guarantees (`internal/identity/service/tokens_test.go:329-355`), and says so rather than reading as an org-wide list.","files":null}]}